{
  "description": "Check whether every run's write-up reports the same, correct moduli.\n\nFor each run of the reproducibility and use-calkit experiments, the elastic\nmodulus its write-up reports for each material is read from the first\ntable row naming the material, or else the first sentence that does: the\nfirst number there in the material's plausible range. Each is compared\nwith the modulus computed here from the data, at the precision the write-up\ngives it. Either of two estimates counts as correct: a fit to all of a\nmaterial's points, or the mean of its specimens' fits, which agree here\nsince every specimen was tested at the same strains.\n\nA reported value that doesn't match is also looked for among the numbers\nin the run's own output files (CSV, JSON, and text), at the same\nprecision, to tell a number the analysis produced from one only in the\nwrite-up, and traced through the run: the write-up's line that reports it,\nthe value its saved script printed (from the session transcript), the\nspecimen moduli as its output files hold them, and any other command whose\noutput gave the reported value to more precision. The write-up, that\noutput file, and those commands with their output are copied to\n``results/numerical-agreement/<run>/`` so they can be cited; each is\ndeclared as an output of this stage in ``calkit.yaml``, since a file inside\nan output directory isn't yet attributed to the stage that writes it.\n\nWhether each write-up was generated by the run's own pipeline, i.e., is an\noutput in its ``dvc.yaml``, so its numbers come from results files rather\nthan being typed in, is recorded too.",
